# ============================================================
# Line endings — one answer for the whole repo.
#
# Without this file, whichever editor or tool touched a file last
# decided its line endings, and a Windows checkout could rewrite every
# text file to CRLF. Git then reported the entire repo as modified:
# 4,300 changed lines across 11 files with not one character of real
# content different. Real changes became impossible to see, and any
# commit made in that state buried a one-line fix under a whole-file
# diff.
#
# `text=auto` tells Git to normalise text files to LF in the object
# store; `eol=lf` tells it to check them back out as LF too, on every
# platform. The second half is the part that matters here — normalising
# only the stored copy still leaves CRLF on disk, which is exactly the
# state that produced the churn.
#
# This is safe on Windows: every tool in this project's path (VS Code,
# Node, wrangler, PowerShell 7+) reads LF without complaint.
# ============================================================

* text=auto eol=lf

# Marked explicitly rather than relying on content sniffing. A binary
# file that Git mistakes for text gets "normalised" — that is, corrupted.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.webp binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.pdf binary
